    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `convert_one_fmt_off_pair`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `convert_one_fmt_off_pair` function processes a node in a syntax tree to handle special formatting directives like `# fmt: off` and `# fmt: skip`. It iterates over the leaves of the node, checking for comments that match these directives. If a valid directive is found, it identifies the nodes that should be ignored based on the directive and constructs a new standalone comment that encapsulates the ignored nodes. This new comment is then inserted back into the tree, preserving the original formatting as much as possible. The function relies on helper functions like `list_comments`, `_contains_fmt_skip_comment`, `generate_ignored_nodes`, and `preceding_leaf` to perform its tasks. It returns `True` if any changes were made, otherwise `False`. The function handles edge cases such as comments before closing brackets and ensures that the original indentation is preserved when applicable. It also includes assertions to catch internal errors related to node handling, which would indicate a bug in the code.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the rol